1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
The processor runs in two modes. A single bit in the status register decides which one, and only the kernel can change it.

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:16,000
An ordinary function call pushes a return address and jumps. Both caller and callee stay in user mode, in the same address space.

3
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:23,000
A system call executes a trap instruction. The hardware switches mode, jumps to a fixed handler, and later returns to the next instruction.

4
00:00:23,000 --> 00:00:30,000
So: same address space, same process, different privilege. That is the difference between a call and a system call.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:38,000
One read of eight bytes from descriptor 3: the number 0 goes in %rax, the descriptor in %rdi, the buffer in %rsi, the count in %rdx.

6
00:00:38,000 --> 00:00:45,000
The trap instruction hands control to the kernel, which indexes its own table with the number and calls the handler for entry 0.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:53,000
The result comes back in %rax. Success returns the count read; failure returns a negative code that the C library converts to -1 and errno.

8
00:00:53,000 --> 00:01:00,000
The trap instruction itself is not privileged — that is why user code may execute it. What it stands in for, reading a disk, is.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:08,000
Three doors lead in: a trap the program executes on purpose, a fault it causes by accident, and an interrupt a device raises.

10
00:01:08,000 --> 00:01:16,000
GATE CSE 2023 Q13 asks which options guarantee a move from user mode to kernel mode: function call, malloc, page fault, system call.

11
00:01:16,000 --> 00:01:23,000
A function call stays in user mode. malloc may be served from the heap without any call. A page fault and a system call always enter the kernel.

12
00:01:23,000 --> 00:01:30,000
Answer key: C and D. The rule behind it is that only an event the hardware must hand to the kernel counts.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:38,000
A mode switch saves the program counter and status word, flips the mode bit, jumps to the handler, and returns. Four steps.

14
00:01:38,000 --> 00:01:46,000
A process switch does all four of those and four more: run the scheduler, save everything, change the page table base, disturb the TLB.

15
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:53,000
GATE CSE 2011 Q6: T1 is the mode switch and T2 the process switch, and the answer is T1 < T2.

16
00:01:53,000 --> 00:02:00,000
The consequence: a program that makes many system calls is slow for a reason that has nothing to do with process switching.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:08,000
printf is a library function. It formats into a buffer the process owns, and only write, the system call, reaches the kernel.

18
00:02:08,000 --> 00:02:16,000
Three rules: stderr is unbuffered, stdout is line buffered on a terminal, and stdout is fully buffered when it is a file or a pipe.

19
00:02:16,000 --> 00:02:23,000
Five lines of six bytes: five write calls on a terminal, one when redirected, because all 30 bytes fit in the buffer until exit.

20
00:02:23,000 --> 00:02:30,000
Two thousand prints of ten bytes: 2000 calls at a terminal against 5 redirected, because 20000 bytes need five full buffers.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:38,000
printf with no newline, redirected to a file: the five bytes are in the process buffer, and nothing has been written yet.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:46,000
fork duplicates the process, and the buffer is part of the process. The child gets its own copy of those unflushed bytes.

23
00:02:46,000 --> 00:02:53,000
Three forks give eight processes, so the five bytes are written eight times. Line buffered, the same program writes them once.

24
00:02:53,000 --> 00:03:00,000
The fix is to flush before forking: end the line, or call fflush. A newline alone is enough only when the stream is line buffered.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:08,000
A shell runs a program in four calls: fork to make a process, exec to put the program in it, exit when it ends, wait to collect it.

26
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:16,000
execve replaces the memory image with a new program and does not return on success. The process identifier is unchanged.

27
00:03:16,000 --> 00:03:23,000
A child that exits but is not waited for stays a zombie. wait collects the status and frees the slot; the status keeps its low eight bits.

28
00:03:23,000 --> 00:03:30,000
GATE CSE 2024 Set 1: the loop with fork, printf and wait prints 14 times. wait changes the order, never the count.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
exec throws away the program: text, data, heap and stack are replaced, and the caught signal handlers go back to their defaults.

30
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:46,000
What survives: the identifier, the parent identifier, the open descriptors, the working directory, the umask, the limits and the identity.

31
00:03:46,000 --> 00:03:53,000
The sharp edge: ignored signals stay ignored, caught signals are reset. That asymmetry is a favourite question.

32
00:03:53,000 --> 00:04:00,000
And the descriptor table survives, which is how a redirection set up before exec still works after it. That is the next scene.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
Two levels: the descriptor table belongs to the process, the open file description belongs to the kernel and holds the offset.

34
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:16,000
fork copies the table but not the descriptions. Both processes read through the same description, so the offsets go 4 then 8.

35
00:04:16,000 --> 00:04:23,000
Two separate open calls make two descriptions, and then each has its own offset from zero. Same file, different question.

36
00:04:23,000 --> 00:04:30,000
exec keeps every descriptor unless it was marked close-on-exec, and dup2 is how a shell makes descriptor 1 point at a file.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:38,000
Run the 2024 program as a model: three iterations, populations of 2, 4 and 8, every one printing once, for 14 executions in 8 processes.

38
00:04:38,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Four traps: printf is not a system call, the trap instruction is not privileged, wait changes order not count, and a shared description shares its offset.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:53,000
The procedure for any question here: name the call, name its mode, then ask what the call changes and what it leaves alone.

40
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:00,000
One boundary, four calls and two tables explain the whole topic. Everything else on this page is a consequence.

